# wb-blockorder — content hashes.  Lane w-read-r8 (read R8).
#
# TWO KINDS OF ENTRY, and only the first kind is load-bearing:
#
#  (a) PREDICTIVE FREEZES — switch_grid.cpp / cells.tsv / gen_grid.py, and
#      holdout_grid.cpp / holdout_pred.tsv.  These were hashed and COMMITTED
#      BEFORE the first `cl.exe`, and holdout_pred.tsv carries predictions made
#      before anything was compiled.  Their value is that the predictions
#      cannot have been written after seeing the objs.
#
#  (b) REPRODUCIBILITY HASHES — random_corpus.cpp / random_cells.tsv.  The
#      randomized corpus was generated AFTER the rule was committed and
#      published, so it is an out-of-sample test of a FIXED rule, not a frozen
#      prediction.  These hashes only certify that the seeded generator
#      reproduces the same 240 cells.
#
#      Honest note: this corpus was regenerated once.  The first version used
#      marker constants up to 60,000, which do not fit a signed-16-bit
#      `li r3,imm` field, so 230 of 240 cells came back UNREADABLE (reported as
#      such, never scored as passes).  The marker range was narrowed to
#      1000..30000 and the corpus regenerated.  The rule under test did not
#      change between the two runs — it was already committed at c1d22e5e9.
#
# Verify from the REPO ROOT:  sha256sum -c docs/whitebox/grids/wb-blockorder/FROZEN.sha256
65c372b6b3a11d8a82d85908af9e5d8c0506a2e508a508456bf7403ac4f54547  docs/whitebox/grids/wb-blockorder/switch_grid.cpp
